{"name":"Imperial Purple","hex":"#5b3167","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"imperial","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Related to an empire, emperor, or empress.","Relating to the British imperial system of measurement."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bottle of wine (usually Bordeaux) containing 6 liters of fluid, eight times the volume of a standard bottle.","A writing paper size measuring 30 × 22 inches, or printing paper measuring 32 × 22 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/imperial"},{"term":"purple","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour between red and blue; violet, though often closer to magenta.","Any non-spectral colour on the line of purples on a colour chromaticity diagram or a colour wheel between violet and red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a purple hue.","Not predominantly red or blue, but having a mixture of Democrat and Republican support."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/purple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tyrian purple","blocks":[{"text":"Tyrian purple, also known as royal purple, imperial purple, imperial dye, or simply tyrian, is a reddish-purple natural dye. It is secreted by several species of predatory sea snails in the family Muricidae, rock snails originally known by the name murex. The coloured compound is 6,6'-dibromoindigo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Purple_Purpur_%28retouched%29.jpg/330px-Purple_Purpur_%28retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tyrian_purple","wikidata":"Q5180360"},{"title":"Purple","blocks":[{"text":"Purple is a color similar in appearance to violet light. In the RYB color model historically used in the arts, purple is a secondary color created by combining red and blue pigments. In the CMYK color model used in modern printing, purple is made by combining magenta pigment with cyan pigment, black pigment, or both. In the RGB color model used in computer and television screens, purple is created by mixing red and blue light in order to create colors that appear similar to violet light. According to color theory, purple is considered a cool color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg/330px-Iris_sanguinea_cultivar%2C_Wakehurst_Place%2C_UK_-_Diliff.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purple","wikidata":"Q3257809"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg/330px-PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg/330px-PM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 20th-century depiction of a Roman triumph celebrated by Julius Caesar. Caesar, riding in the chariot, wears the solid Tyrian purple toga picta. In the foreground, two Roman magistrates are identified by their toga praetexta, white with a stripe of Tyrian purple.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APM_110511_Liebig_Chromos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg/500px-Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg/1280px-Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg/500px-Haustellum_brandaris_000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two shells of Bolinus brandari